    {
      "id": "xWtlcXc-If5TIcn8y2dTY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2392.666666666669,
      "y": -1277.1111195882163,
      "width": 2744.2799999999993,
      "height": 315.00000000000006,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 140359651,
      "version": 85,
      "versionNonce": 1592790477,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790587925142,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Does the problem have overlapping subproblems?\nMeaning: while solving the problem, do you end up solving the same smaller problem again and again?\n\n2. Can the answer be built from answers to smaller problems?\nMeaning: answer(n) can be calculated using something like answer(n-1), answer(n-2), etc.",
      "fontSize": 50.39999999999998,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Does the problem have overlapping subproblems?\nMeaning: while solving the problem, do you end up solving the same smaller problem again and again?\n\n2. Can the answer be built from answers to smaller problems?\nMeaning: answer(n) can be calculated using something like answer(n-1), answer(n-2), etc.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8Hnzuo3Hu25KJyq43Xgoa",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 7.333333333331211,
      "y": -3147.1111195882163,
      "width": 2079,
      "height": 188.99999999999991,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a44",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1352388296,
      "version": 174,
      "versionNonce": 1041866936,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790670036446,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. Divide :- Break the problem into smaller subproblems.\n2. Conquer :- Solve those smaller subproblems, usually using recursion.\n3. Combine :-Combine their results to get the answer to the original problem.",
      "fontSize": 50.39999999999998,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. Divide :- Break the problem into smaller subproblems.\n2. Conquer :- Solve those smaller subproblems, usually using recursion.\n3. Combine :-Combine their results to get the answer to the original problem.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "cfwhkyHiwnDyYsrOV2opm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -6794.333333333335,
      "y": -3178.7777862548833,
      "width": 3628.3333333333344,
      "height": 1007.9999999999995,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a48",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 778441912,
      "version": 1729,
      "versionNonce": 647571400,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790669870337,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "If an array has length n, then the total number of possible subarrays is: n * (n + 1) / 2\n\nFor the Maximum Subarray Sum problem, we can use Kadane's Algorithm. Let's understand it with an example:[-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1]\nThe idea is to start with nums[0] and maintain a currSum. We keep adding every number to `currSum`. The important part is: If currSum\nbecomes negative, we reset it to 0.\n\nWhy? Because a negative sum will never help us build a maximum-sum subarray.For example: -1 + 1 + (-3) = -3\n\nNow if we continue with 4: -3 + 4 = 1 . We are better off starting directly from 4 instead of carrying the previous negative sum. So\nwhen currSum becomes negative, we simply reset: currSum = 0 and start building the next subarray from the next element. For this\nexample: [-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1] start with 4 --> 4 + (-1) = 3 --> 3 + 2    = 5 --> 5 + 1 = 6 So the maximum subarray is: [4, -1, 2,\n1] and its maximum sum is: 6\n\nThe main idea to remember is:\nIf my current sum becomes negative, it is only going to hurt the next subarray, so discard it and start fresh.\n",
      "fontSize": 50.39999999999998,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "If an array has length n, then the total number of possible subarrays is: n * (n + 1) / 2\n\nFor the Maximum Subarray Sum problem, we can use Kadane's Algorithm. Let's understand it with an example:[-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1]\nThe idea is to start with nums[0] and maintain a currSum. We keep adding every number to `currSum`. The important part is: If currSum becomes negative, we reset it to 0.\n\nWhy? Because a negative sum will never help us build a maximum-sum subarray.For example: -1 + 1 + (-3) = -3\n\nNow if we continue with 4: -3 + 4 = 1 . We are better off starting directly from 4 instead of carrying the previous negative sum. So when currSum becomes negative, we simply reset: currSum = 0 and start building the next subarray from the next element. For this example: [-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1] start with 4 --> 4 + (-1) = 3 --> 3 + 2    = 5 --> 5 + 1 = 6 So the maximum subarray is: [4, -1, 2, 1] and its maximum sum is: 6\n\nThe main idea to remember is:\nIf my current sum becomes negative, it is only going to hurt the next subarray, so discard it and start fresh.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "D5aGex_HJzSpyZ08IEY0T",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -6814.333333333335,
      "y": -1823.7777862548833,
      "width": 3628.3333333333344,
      "height": 1007.9999999999995,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4C",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1689632491,
      "version": 1791,
      "versionNonce": 1099357003,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790848240355,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "If an array has length n, then the total number of possible subarrays is: n * (n + 1) / 2\n\nFor the Maximum Subarray Sum problem, we can use Kadane's Algorithm. Let's understand it with an example:[-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1]\nThe idea is to start with nums[0] and maintain a currSum. We keep adding every number to `currSum`. The important part is: If currSum\nbecomes negative, we reset it to 0.\n\nWhy? Because a negative sum will never help us build a maximum-sum subarray.For example: -1 + 1 + (-3) = -3\n\nNow if we continue with 4: -3 + 4 = 1 . We are better off starting directly from 4 instead of carrying the previous negative sum. So\nwhen currSum becomes negative, we simply reset: currSum = 0 and start building the next subarray from the next element. For this\nexample: [-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1] start with 4 --> 4 + (-1) = 3 --> 3 + 2    = 5 --> 5 + 1 = 6 So the maximum subarray is: [4, -1, 2,\n1] and its maximum sum is: 6\n\nThe main idea to remember is:\nIf my current sum becomes negative, it is only going to hurt the next subarray, so discard it and start fresh.\n",
      "fontSize": 50.39999999999998,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "If an array has length n, then the total number of possible subarrays is: n * (n + 1) / 2\n\nFor the Maximum Subarray Sum problem, we can use Kadane's Algorithm. Let's understand it with an example:[-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1]\nThe idea is to start with nums[0] and maintain a currSum. We keep adding every number to `currSum`. The important part is: If currSum becomes negative, we reset it to 0.\n\nWhy? Because a negative sum will never help us build a maximum-sum subarray.For example: -1 + 1 + (-3) = -3\n\nNow if we continue with 4: -3 + 4 = 1 . We are better off starting directly from 4 instead of carrying the previous negative sum. So when currSum becomes negative, we simply reset: currSum = 0 and start building the next subarray from the next element. For this example: [-1, 1, -3, 4, -1, 2, 1] start with 4 --> 4 + (-1) = 3 --> 3 + 2    = 5 --> 5 + 1 = 6 So the maximum subarray is: [4, -1, 2, 1] and its maximum sum is: 6\n\nThe main idea to remember is:\nIf my current sum becomes negative, it is only going to hurt the next subarray, so discard it and start fresh.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ViftjfbVe9Fznwl8n4lKh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2402.666666666669,
      "y": -3217.1111195882163,
      "width": 1933.3333333333335,
      "height": 188.99999999999991,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 43375469,
      "version": 250,
      "versionNonce": 399292771,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790590199050,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Ask yourself: -\n\"For index i, do I need some information about everything before i or\neverything after i?\"",
      "fontSize": 50.39999999999998,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Ask yourself: -\n\"For index i, do I need some information about everything before i or everything after i?\"",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "GAYqlPqX8jz18L2Ah--ct",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3829.0793650793617,
      "y": -1457.1111195882163,
      "width": 2130.8412698412703,
      "height": 190.07003027771967,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a4V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 255135907,
      "version": 810,
      "versionNonce": 1000951405,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790587943097,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Every opertation have same TC only the difference is when we do not need a\nvalue with the key and do not need duplicates then use set otherwise use\nHash table",
      "fontSize": 50.68534140739191,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Every opertation have same TC only the difference is when we do not need a value with the key and do not need duplicates then use set otherwise use Hash table",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "ArorG7H4y4B7uFneVOzKb",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2227.333333333331,
      "y": -1302.1111195882163,
      "width": 1174,
      "height": 251.9999999999999,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a5",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 961540685,
      "version": 297,
      "versionNonce": 1210280429,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790587901783,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The strongest question to ask yourself :- \nIf I make the best choice available right\nnow, can I prove that I will never regret\nthat choice later?",
      "fontSize": 50.39999999999998,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The strongest question to ask yourself :- \nIf I make the best choice available right now, can I prove that I will never regret that choice later?",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
